-- title:
--   Integral Laurent series over i(R)-coefficient subrings have R-coefficients
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring which is a domain and a principal ideal ring, let $K$ be a field, and let $i \colon R \to K$ be an injective ring homomorphism. Let $x$ be a Laurent series over $K$, i.e. an element of `LaurentSeries K`, subject to two conditions. First, $x$ lies in the subfield of `LaurentSeries K` generated by the image of the ring homomorphism `PowerSeries R → LaurentSeries K` obtained by applying $i$ to all coefficients, via `PowerSeries.map i`, followed by the inclusion `HahnSeries.ofPowerSeries ℤ K` of power series into Laurent series; that is, $x$ is a subfield-theoretic combination of power series all of whose coefficients lie in $i(R)$. Second, there is a subring $S$ of `LaurentSeries K` each of whose elements $z$ has every coefficient $z_n$, $n \in \mathbb{Z}$, in the set-theoretic range of $i$, and $x$ is integral over $S$. The conclusion is that $x$ is the coefficientwise image of a Laurent series over $R$: there exists $y$ in `LaurentSeries R` such that $i(y_n) = x_n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{Z}$.
--
--   This is the integral-closedness ingredient of the $q$-expansion principle, in the form that a Laurent series generated by $i(R)$-coefficient power series and integral over a subring with $i(R)$-coefficients already has all its coefficients in $i(R)$, the underlying algebraic input being that $R[[q]]$ is integrally closed for $R$ a principal ideal domain. It is used in the study of $q$-expansions of functions on modular curves, where it bounds the residue field of a function-field point by the algebra generated by the coefficientwise reduction of a $q$-expansion.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_LaurentSeries_exists_forall_coeff_eq_of_isIntegral_of_mem_closure_range_ofPowerSeries.lean

